<Article
  title="What Is Horsepower? Air Conditioner Capacity Explained"
  description="Why horsepower is a misleading way to compare air conditioners, and why you should always compare capacity in kW instead."
  crumb="What is horsepower?"
  heading="What is horsepower, and why doesn’t it matter?"
  lede="Retailers still quote air conditioners in horsepower. Here’s why that number can’t tell you how much heating or cooling you’re getting, and what to ask for instead."
  photo="outdoorUnitBracket"
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="meaning">What does horsepower mean for an air conditioner?</h2>
  <p>
    The Australian Government’s Energy Rating website (<a href="https://www.energyrating.gov.au/" rel="noopener">energyrating.gov.au</a>)
    answers this directly:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      “The ongoing use of horsepower by retailers is of concern to energy regulators in Australia. This term bears no relation
      to the output capacity of the unit. The only valid capacity measure is the kW output under AS/NZS3823 condition T1 for
      cooling or H1 for heating.”
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The term comes from a time when air conditioner compressors were driven by mechanical motors. Today, how much heating or
    cooling a unit delivers depends on the efficiency of its motor and compressor, the design of its evaporator and condenser,
    airflow, fan efficiency, the control system and more. Using the power of the electric motor as a stand-in for capacity is
    misleading in every case.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kw">Why we quote in kW</h2>
  <p>
    At Factory Direct Home Air Conditioning we always quote the capacity of a unit in kW. A unit with poor efficiency might put
    out 2.0 kW and be marketed as “1 horsepower”, while a high-efficiency unit putting out 2.7 kW could be marketed as
    “1 horsepower” too.
  </p>

  <HorsepowerChart />

  <h2 id="compare">How to compare quotes</h2>
  <p>
    It’s hard to compare air conditioners by horsepower, but easy to compare them by kilowatts. If another retailer quotes you
    in horsepower, ask them for the capacity in kW so you can make an accurate comparison.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <Icon name="triangle-alert" />
    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> compare the kW output for cooling and for heating. They’re usually different numbers.</p>
  </div>
</Article>
